mes>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Nonlinear protein degradation and the function of genetic circuits.</article-title><source>Proceedings of the National Academy of Science USA</source><volume>102</volume><fpage>9559</fpage><lpage>9564</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Eiing1"><label>22</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ei&#x000df;ing</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Allg&#x000f6;wer</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Bullinger</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Robustness properties of apoptosis models with respect to parameter variations and intrinsic noise.</article-title><source>IEEE Proceedings on Systems Biology</source><volume>152</volume><fpage>221</fpage><lpage>228</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Covert1"><label>23</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Covert</surname><given-names>MW</given-names></name><name><surname>Schilling</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name><name><surname>Palsson</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Regulation of gene expression in flux balance models of metabolism.</article-title><source>Journal of Theoretical Biology</source><volume>213</volume><fpage>73</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11708855</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Lee1"><label>24</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Gianchandani</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name><name><surname>Papin</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Flux balance analysis in the era of metabolomics.</article-title><source>Briefings in Bioinformatics</source><volume>7</volume><fpage>140</fpage><lpage>150</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16772264</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Reed1"><label>25</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reed</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Pellecchia</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Apoptosis-based therapies for hematologic malignancies.</article-title><source>Blood</source><volume>106</volume><fpage>408</fpage><lpage>418</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15797997</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Albert1"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Albert</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Othmer</surname><given-names>HG</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>The topology of the regulatory interactions predicts the expression pattern of the segment polarity genes in drosophila melanogaster.</article-title><source>Journal of Theoretical Biology</source><volume>223</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>18</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12782112</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Duarte1"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Duarte</surname><given-names>NC</given-names></name><name><surname>Becker</surname><given-names>SA</given-names></name><name><surname>Jamshidi</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Thiele</surname><given-names>NI</given-names></name><name><surname>Mo</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Global reconstruction of the human metabolic network based on genomic and bibliomic data.</article-title><source>Proceedings of the National Academy of Science U S A</source><volume>104</volume><fpage>1777</fpage><lpage>1782</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Kauffman1"><label>28</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kauffman</surname><given-names>SA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1993</year><source>The origins of order: Self-organization and selection in evolution.</source><publisher-name>Oxford University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Weiss1"><label>29</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>JN</given-names></name></person-group><year>1997</year><article-title>The hill equation revisited: uses and misuses.</article-title><source>FASEB Journal</source><volume>11</volume><fpage>835</fpage><lpage>841</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9285481</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Cormen1"><label>30</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cormen</surname><given-names>TH</given-names></name><name><surname>Leiserson</surname><given-names>CE</given-names></name><name><surname>Rivest</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Stein</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><year>2001</year><source>Introduction to Algorithms.</source><publisher-name>MIT Press and McGraw-Hill</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Feng1"><label>31</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>XJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Rabitz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Optimal identification of biochemical reaction networks.</article-title><source>Biophysical Journal</source><volume>86</volume><fpage>1270</fpage><lpage>1281</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14990460</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Hooshangi1"><label>32</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hooshangi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>XJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Rabitz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Optimizing genetic circuits by global sensitivity analysis.</article-title><source>Biophysical Journal</source><volume>87</volume><fpage>2195</fpage><lpage>2202</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15454422</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000547-Ku1"><label>33</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ku</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Rabitz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Closed-loop learning control of bio-networks.</article-title><source>Journal of Computational Biology</source><volume>11</volume><fpage>642</fpage><lpage>659</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15579236</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig id="pone-0000547-g001" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g001</object-id><label>Figure 1</label><caption><title>The human apoptosis network modified, as described in the text, from that in the Kegg database (hsa04210).</title><p>There are 47 genes in the figure and an additional node which we label the output node. The 47 genes may be catagorized (see <xref ref-type="table" rid="pone-0000547-t001">Table 1</xref>) as: input genes (dashed circles); membrane genes; death genes; life genes; and finally the remaining genes in the network. The output, or &#x0201c;death&#x0201d; node (48) is added to represent the cumulative effect of many genes implicated in the onset of the cell death machinery. In this figure, solid lines indicate promotion while short dashed links indicate inhibition. The long dashed link between CASP3 and CASP8 adds the possibility of feedback in the apoptosome.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g001"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g002" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g002</object-id><label>Figure 2</label><caption><title>The distribution of gene activities in heterogeneous cell populations with a population size of 50,000.</title><p>These distribution are for majority rule signaling with positive and continuous gene activities. a) The activity of the output node is close to a normal distribution due to many pathways arriving at the output node; b) Signal statistics at the gene NFkB, which is an internal node lying at the end of a chain of links (see <xref ref-type="fig" rid="pone-0000547-g001">Fig. 1</xref>), is close to log-normal. (see <xref ref-type="sec" rid="s3">discussion</xref> in the text).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g002"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g003" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g003</object-id><label>Figure 3</label><caption><title>A parallel combination of signaling pathways with no series connections.</title><p>For a large number of parallel connections, the output activity <italic>a<sub>out</sub></italic> is normally distributed (see text).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g003"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g004" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g004</object-id><label>Figure 4</label><caption><title>A signaling pathway with four steps in series and with no parallel connections.</title><p>If the number of steps in the pathway is large, the ouput activity <italic>a<sub>out</sub></italic> obeys log-normal statistics (see text).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g004"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g005" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g005</object-id><label>Figure 5</label><caption><title>Majority rule signaling with non-linearity.</title><p>The distribution of signals in heterogeneous cell populations with a population size of 50,000. These distribution are for majority rule signaling with positive, continuous gene activities calculated from the signal by using the Michaelis-Menten law <italic>a</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;<italic>s</italic>/(1&#x0002b;<italic>s</italic>). a) Signal statistics at the output node is similar to a normal distribution; b) Signal statistics at the gene NFkB remains close to log-normal despite the non-linear dependence of the activity on the signal.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g005"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g006" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g006</object-id><label>Figure 6</label><caption><title>Feedback loop in the apoptosis network, following </title><p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="pone.0000547-Bagci1">&#x0005b;20&#x0005d;</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g006"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g007" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g007</object-id><label>Figure 7</label><caption><title>The steady state activity of genes in the feedback loop illustrated in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="pone-0000547-g006">Fig. 6</xref>.</title><p>The top figure is the case of linear signaling (<italic>d</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0, <italic>c</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;<italic>b</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1 in Eq. (10)), the middle figure is for the Michaelis-Menten case (<italic>b</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;<italic>c</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;<italic>d</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1 in Eq. (10)), and the bottom figure was found using Eq. (10) with <italic>c</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2, <italic>d</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1 and <italic>b</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2 corresponding to co-operative non-linearity. In the top figure the link weights are &#x003c9;<sub>12</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.367, &#x003c9;<sub>23</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.864, &#x003c9;<sub>34</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.754, &#x003c9;<sub>45</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.617, &#x003c9;<sub>51</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.718, &#x003c9;<sub>51</sub>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;0.828. In the middle and bottom figures the weights used were (0.417,0.847,0.287,0.456,0.614,0.521) and (1.812,1,207, 0.971,1.158, 1.924,1.489) respectively. The link weights were chosen to be random on the interval &#x0005b;0,<italic>c</italic>&#x0005d;.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g007"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g008" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g008</object-id><label>Figure 8</label><caption><title>Death statistics in a heterogeneous population of 50,000 cells with co-operative non-linearity and feedback.</title><p>The non-linearity parameters used in Eq. (10) were <italic>c</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1.8, <italic>d</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1, <italic>b</italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2 and all of the link weights were chosen to be random on the interval &#x0005b;0,<italic>c</italic>&#x0005d;.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g008"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g009" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g009</object-id><label>Figure 9</label><caption><title>Discrete signal death statistics.</title><p>a) Relations between the normalized signal, <italic>s<sub>l</sub></italic>/<italic>n</italic>(<italic>l</italic>), arriving at a gene and it's discrete activity, from the top the behaviors are for the logarithmic function, Eq. (13), the sigmoidal function, Eq. (14) and the linear function, Eq. (12). The death statistics for these three models: b) linear function; c) sigmoidal function; d) logarithmic function. It is clear that the distribution of death activities becomes bimodal and broader as we go from the linear function to the logarithmic function.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g009"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g010" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g010</object-id><label>Figure 10</label><caption><title>Distribution of nodes entering in selective combinations for the Linear (dotted), Sigmoid (dashed), and Logarithmic (solid) OSA rules.</title></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g010"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g011" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g011</object-id><label>Figure 11</label><caption><title>Distribution of nodes appearing in selective and mortal perturbations in the linear (top panel) and logarithmic (lower panel) OSA rules.</title><p>The life/death threshold is set to <italic>&#x00073;&#x00305;</italic><italic><sub>o</sub></italic>&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1 for the two rules. The correlation between the mortality and selectivity distributions is 0.88 for the linear case and &#x02212;.80 for logarithmic case.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g011"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g012" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g012</object-id><label>Figure 12</label><caption><title>Correlation matrix for nodes appearing in the same selective perturbation in the linear OSA rule.</title><p>Darker dots indicate that the two nodes given by the row and column of the matrix appear more often in the selective drug combinations.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g012"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g013" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g013</object-id><label>Figure 13</label><caption><title>Correlation matrix for nodes appearing in the same selective perturbation in the sigmoidal OSA rule.</title><p>Darker dots indicate that the two nodes given by the row and column of the matrix appear more often in the selective drug combinations.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g013"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g014" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g014</object-id><label>Figure 14</label><caption><title>Correlation matrix for nodes appearing in the same selective perturbation in the logarithmic OSA rule.</title><p>Darker dots indicate that the two nodes given by the row and column of the matrix appear more often in the selective drug combinations.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g014"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g015" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g015</object-id><label>Figure 15</label><caption><title>Output signal as a function of the perturbation strength on a pro-apoptosis (BAD, red increasing line) and anti-apoptosis node (IkBa, green decreasing line).</title><p>The dashed line and the solid line refers to two different individuals.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g015"/></fig><fig id="pone-0000547-g016" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.g016</object-id><label>Figure 16</label><caption><title>Distribution of nodes entering in selective combinations obtained with the Linear OSA (dotted), sigmoid (dashed) and with the effective linear programming method (solid line).</title></caption><graphic xlink:href="pone.0000547.g016"/></fig><table-wrap id="pone-0000547-t001" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.t001</object-id><label>Table 1</label><caption><title>Typical roles of genes in the signaling network.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0000547-t001-1" xlink:href="pone.0000547.t001"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0000547-t001-1"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">life</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">death</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">membrane</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BIRC2..</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BAD</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FAS</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BCL2L1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CASP6</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">FADD</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BCL2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CASP3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TRADD</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BCL2..</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CASP7</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TNF-R1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BAX</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">RIPK1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">DFFB</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TRAF2</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AIF</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TRAIL-R</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TP53</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IL1R1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MYD88</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IRAK1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NGF&#x003b2;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">TrkA</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IL3</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IL-3R</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pone-0000547-t002" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0000547.t002</object-id><label>Table 2</label><caption><title>Number of individuals that can be selectively controlled in 10 populations with 100 individuals each.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0000547-t002-2" xlink:href="pone.0000547.t002"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0000547-t002-2"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td colspan="3" align="left" rowspan="1">Linear</td><td colspan="3" align="left" rowspan="1">Sigmoid</td><td colspan="3" align="left" rowspan="1">Logarithmic</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">k&#x0200a;&#x0003d;&#x0200a;3</td></tr>